// Allocators (design.md "Allocators", "Build-selected providers").
//
// One provider serves the whole program, reached through
// `mem.default_allocator()`. With nothing selected the runtime supplies the
// system heap; the root package may name a factory for the slot with
// `@(default_allocator = "<import path>:<name>")`. Either way the handle ABI is
// the same: everything a caller holds is one pointer to one provider record.
//
// `mem.Arena` and `mem.Scratch` are the two local region roots, which is what
// makes a `free_all` able to succeed at all.
@(public)
package mem;

// The compiler contributes:
//
//   Allocator          the pointer-sized provider handle
//   Allocator_Error    the allocation-failure result
//   default_allocator  the build-selected default provider
//
// `default_allocator` is the same symbol a generated copy operation's default
// argument names, so an omitted allocator and a written
// `mem.default_allocator()` select one provider through one call.
//
//   Arena              a local bump region, optionally over a caller's buffer
//   Scratch            the same, always provider-backed
//   try_arena          the two constructors again, reporting an allocation
//   try_scratch        failure instead of applying the provider's policy to it
//
// The two types are compiler-owned because the region lattice has to
// *recognise* them, not merely call them: a region created here is one this
// body may reset without a promise, and one no owner may outlive.
//
//   arena := mem.Arena.init();                 // blocks from the default provider
//   arena := mem.Arena.from_buffer(buffer[:]); // caller storage, no allocation
//   scratch := mem.Scratch.init();
//   outcome := mem.try_arena();                // Result(Arena, Allocator_Error)
//
// A reset needs every borrower of the region to be gone, and that is a scope
// rather than a convention — resetting while one is live is rejected (L0537,
// "this reset would end the region backing `xs`, which is still live here"):
//
//   {
//       xs: [dynamic]int via arena.allocator() = {};
//       xs.append(1, 2, 3);
//   }
//   free_all(arena.allocator());        // reusable: the region stays usable
//
// `from_buffer` needs room for the control block, one block header, and the
// slack to align the first of them — 128 bytes on x86-64. A buffer under that ends the
// process rather than failing, because no allocation policy could make it
// succeed, and there is no fallible spelling to reach for: `try_arena` and
// `try_scratch` take a parent allocator, not a buffer. The runtime knows the
// exact figure; it is not published here until a caller has to compute a buffer
// size rather than pick one.
//
// The zero `Arena` or `Scratch` owns an empty region: allocating from it fails
// under the provider's policy, and resetting or dropping it does nothing. That
// is what a `static` provider holds until a factory assigns it.
//
// A provider is move-only: two owners of one control block would release it
// twice, and a bump region has no meaningful copy. Moving one never changes its
// record address, so its region identity survives the move.
